WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.659 --> 00:00:05.450
Welcome to this AI Coffee Break!

00:00:05.450 --> 00:00:16.189
Today we will follow the development of Transformers
for image recognition even further, by discussing

00:00:16.189 --> 00:00:21.020
the Transformer in Transformer paper!
It's fresh out on ArXiv!

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:26.820
This paper comes to alleviate the issue that
Transformers on images have so far handled

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:33.020
the image as a sequence of patches
without modelling the structure inside the

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:36.790
patch.
So this paper puts a Transformer into the

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:43.440
Transformer to do this job!? Yeah, and it
beats the DeiT Transformer model on ImageNet

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:48.989
with similar computational cost.
In this video, we will show you how.

00:00:48.989 --> 00:00:53.850
If you want Ms. Coffee Bean to give you a
quick reminder about Transformer models for

00:00:53.850 --> 00:01:00.800
image recognition so far, check out the ViT
video that shows how transformers can beat

00:01:00.800 --> 00:01:07.550
ImageNet state of the art with a lot of data,
and the DeiT video showing how transformers

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:13.660
can be made more data-efficient so that they
do not need such huge amounts of data.

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:19.800
Find the links in the description below.
So today’s story begins with the DeiT Transformer

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:27.289
from Facebook achieving impressive results
on ImageNet with 86,4M parameters and 17,6B

00:01:27.289 --> 00:01:31.200
FLOPs improving the data-efficiency of ViT
from Google.

00:01:31.200 --> 00:01:34.240
Is there anything more to be to be done than
that?

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:41.289
Well, Vision Transformers so far have been
splitting the image into a sequence of patches.

00:01:41.289 --> 00:01:47.090
But Transformers need vectors, not 2d patches,
therefore the patches are transformed into

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:50.660
an image vector by a linear projection for
example.

00:01:50.660 --> 00:01:56.330
But here, in the transformation from a 2d
patch to a vector, the internal pixel structure

00:01:56.330 --> 00:02:01.880
of the patch is lost.
So it is time for the Transformer in Transformer,

00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:09.390
or short TNT, to come in and model both local
and global information.

00:02:09.390 --> 00:02:14.990
The authors of TNT also split the image into
a sequence of patches. These patches are p

00:02:14.990 --> 00:02:20.650
by p pixels,
where p is set to 16, for example. So until

00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:26.170
here everything is like we are used to from
ViT or DeiT.

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:32.890
But now is where the crazy part happens:
For TNT the authors reshape each patch to

00:02:32.890 --> 00:02:38.260
a super-pixel sequence!!!
A what? Let’s take it slow.

00:02:38.260 --> 00:02:45.100
So the idea of TNT is to model patches better
than just cramping them into a single vector.

00:02:45.100 --> 00:02:50.600
For this, each patch that is actually of stack
of 3 matrices for red, green and blue, is

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:57.850
transformed to something else,
which is a tensor of p’ times p’ and c

00:02:57.850 --> 00:03:02.900
channels. P prime is chosen to be four and
even though Ms. Coffee Bean could not find

00:03:02.900 --> 00:03:08.000
this explicitly written in the paper, she
guesses that this c under the assumption of

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:16.460
no information loss has to be something like
p x p x 3 initial channels divided by p' times

00:03:16.460 --> 00:03:18.700
p'.
Just a hunch.

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:26.180
Then, if p’ equals 2 this new tensor can
be regarded as a set of 4 pixels which are

00:03:26.180 --> 00:03:31.430
now superpixels, because they already contain
information about their neighboring pixels.

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:36.710
Remember, we reduced from p to p' and p is
bigger than p'.

00:03:36.710 --> 00:03:41.490
In reality, these superpixels visualized for
the whole image look like this: So now you

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:45.569
see where this is going.
We have a sequence of superpixels

00:03:45.569 --> 00:03:50.390
that represent a patch and
they themselves are actually vectors,

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:53.680
see?
So we have here two sequences at different

00:03:53.680 --> 00:03:57.599
levels of granularity.
We have the pixel sequence representing a

00:03:57.599 --> 00:04:00.319
patch
and the patch sequence representing the whole

00:04:00.319 --> 00:04:03.410
image.
So we have an inner Transformer block that,

00:04:03.410 --> 00:04:08.280
you know, does self-attention transformer
magic on the pixel level.

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:13.090
Then we have an outer Transformer block on
image patch level, where the image patches

00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:18.590
are represented by a vector in the same way
previous work did. And now the inner and outer

00:04:18.590 --> 00:04:22.310
transformer are combined: What comes out of
the inner transformer

00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:26.970
is concatenated
and then linearly projected to fit the dimension

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:32.660
of the outer Transformer representation, which
is then added to the patch embedding before

00:04:32.660 --> 00:04:39.280
it is processed by the outer transformer.
So the outer transformer now works on patch

00:04:39.280 --> 00:04:44.720
level and on the representations that
were computed by the inner transformer on

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:47.940
the pixel level.
And of course, what we have visualized here

00:04:47.940 --> 00:04:53.030
for one patch, is done for all. And we have
also not shown the positional embeddings,

00:04:53.030 --> 00:04:57.990
which are present at both patch level and
pixel level for the Transformer to have an

00:04:57.990 --> 00:05:03.740
idea about the location of things in the image,
which is not clear from the set of vectors

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:07.180
alone.
And this was it for the Transformer in Transformer!

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:11.280
We strongly recommend you to check out the
paper for really cool stuff.

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:16.590
You can see not only that the TNT features
are richer and meaningful for human judgement,

00:05:16.590 --> 00:05:21.910
but you also see DeiT features. I always wondered
how they look like!

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:27.320
You can read in the paper see how the learnt
features by TNT transfer to other benchmark

00:05:27.320 --> 00:05:30.300
datasets.
It's a crazy time for the Transformers!

00:05:30.300 --> 00:05:37.100
They do this, they do that, they are everywhere.
